//! Terrain: textured biome tiles, hills, mountains, features, natural
//! wonders, cliffs, shores, and rivers.
//!
//! The art lives in `assets/terrain/*.png` (generated by `tools/terrain`):
//!   ground.png    8x4 cells: 8 biomes x 3 variants, then rock, sand, white
//!   relief.png    hills (0-14) and mountains (15-29) per land biome
//!   features.png  forest, rainforest, taiga, marsh, floodplains, dunes,
//!                 glacier, oasis, sea ice
//!   wonders.png   one sprite per natural wonder (+ a generic marker)
//!   doodads.png   small ground details (grass, flowers, rocks, scrub...)
//! Missing files fall back to the flat-colour drawing in `flat`.
//!
//! To keep thousands of tiles cheap, tiles are not painted one by one:
//! every `draw_tile` appends to four per-frame mesh batches (ground,
//! fringes and shores, rivers, sprites) that are reserved in the painter
//! on the first tile and handed over by the first `draw_river` call, which
//! `map_view` makes after the last `draw_tile` of the frame.
//!
//! Water is alive: coast foam laps in and out, glints drift over the sea, and
//! rivers carry travelling highlights downstream. All of it is baked into the
//! same batches (a few extra vertices per water tile, only when zoomed in
//! enough to see them) and driven by the clock `map_view` hands over.

mod atlas;
mod flat;

